      {
        "id": "watch-01",
        "startMs": 0,
        "text": "For the same items measured twice, first compute one difference per item. Here use the nine differences, not two independent lists.",
        "endMs": 5400
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-02",
        "startMs": 5400,
        "text": "Population SD is unknown. On Calculator, choose Menu → 6 Statistics → 7 Stat Tests → 2 t Test. Switch Data Input Method to Stats.",
        "endMs": 13100
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-03",
        "startMs": 13100,
        "text": "Use μ₀ = 0, x̄ = 3, sx = 4 and n = 9. The claim is an increase: select μ > μ₀ and confirm.",
        "endMs": 25800
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-04",
        "startMs": 25800,
        "text": "t = 2.25, df = 8. The one-sided PVal is about 0.02728. At 5%, reject H₀: there is evidence the mean difference is positive.",
        "endMs": 30400
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-05",
        "startMs": 30400,
        "text": "For independent normal groups with equal population variances, choose Stat Tests → 4 2-Sample t Test and switch to Stats.",
        "endMs": 38150
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-06",
        "startMs": 38150,
        "text": "Group 1: x̄ = 35, sx = 4, n = 10. Group 2: x̄ = 31, sx = 5, n = 12. Choose μ₁ ≠ μ₂ and Pooled: Yes.",
        "endMs": 53100
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-07",
        "startMs": 53100,
        "text": "The two-sided PVal is about 0.0547, above 0.05. Do not reject H₀; that is not evidence the two means are equal.",
        "endMs": 58000
      }
    ],
